This project implements an end-to-end equity analysis workflow that combines financial theory with data analytics to 
assess risk and expected returns across publicly traded US stocks. Market and company-level data are retrieved programmatically (through the usage of Finnhub API),
transformed into economically meaningful metrics, and stored in a structured database for scalable analysis.

Using the Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM), expected returns are calculated for individual equities based on their exposure 
to systematic market risk (beta). Market returns are derived from historical S&P 500 data using monthly geometric returns to ensure
statistically robust assumptions. The processed results are stored in a relational SQL database, enabling clean data management and seamless integration with business intelligence tools.

The final analysis is conducted in Power BI, where interactive visualizations are used to explore the relationship between risk 
and return. Scatter plots highlight the positioning of stocks relative to market risk, quadrant analysis supports comparative evaluation,
and distribution charts reveal broader market patterns in beta and CAPM-implied returns. Together, these views provide both stock-level and market-level insights, 
supporting data-driven investment reasoning and portfolio analysis.
